    "created_at": "2020-04-14T14:26:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-04-14T14:26:42Z",
    "body": "My workplace just switched from Subversion to Git, so I probably won't be pushing too hard for this (I'm also not using starship on a regular basis haha).\r\n\r\nI think the first step should be to figure out how starship wants to organize and store the data. Right now, there is a `repo` field that is pretty Git-specific. So we would need to decide if we want to rename that to `git_repo` or just leave it as `repo` and have a Subversion-specific `svn_repo`.\r\n\r\nOnce that has been decided, you'll need to get the information from `svn`. I haven't looked too much into it, but I assume there's a good XML parser written in Rust. It would probably be more reliable and less fragile to query Subversion with the `--xml` flag (e.g. `svn info --xml`) and parse the XML.\r\n\r\nYou could also look into parsing the output of `svn status --xml` to see whether or not there are untracked, modified, or deleted files, like Git's default PS1 shows with `?`, `!`, etc.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
